"Bounded Twin-Width Tournaments are $\\dchi$-Bounded (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02763)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Date: Wed Sep  2 16:04:27 2026\n\nFor a tournament $T$ and a vertex ordering $\\prec$, let $T^{\\prec}$ be the graph of backward arcs in $\\prec$. ... Together with polynomial $χ$-boundedness of graphs of bounded twin-width, this implies that tournaments of bounded twin-width are $\\dchi$-bounded by $\\domega$, resolving a conjecture of Aboulker, Aubian, Charbit, and Lopes.\nTitle: Bounded Twin-Width Tournaments are $\\dchi$-Bounded\nAuthors: Chaoliang Tang, Junchi Zhang\nDate: Wed Sep  2 16:04:27 2026\n\nFor a tournament $T$ and a vertex ordering $\\prec$, let $T^{\\prec}$ be the graph of backward arcs in $\\prec$. The diclique number of a tournament is $\\domega(T)=\\min_{\\prec}ω(T^{\\prec})$, and the dichromatic number is $\\dchi(T)=\\min_{\\prec}χ(T^{\\prec})$. We prove a mixed parameter transfer theorem: for all $k$ and $r$, if $\\tww(T)\\le k$ and $ω(T^{\\prec})\\le r$, then the ordered twin-width of $(T^{\\prec},\\prec)$ is bounded by a function of $k$ and $r$. The proof combines the regular-semigrid theorem for ordered graphs with permutation-encoding obstructions to bounded twin-width in tournaments. Together with polynomial $χ$-boundedness of graphs of bounded twin-width, this implies that tournaments of bounded twin-width are $\\dchi$-bounded by $\\domega$, resolving a conjecture of Aboulker, Aubian, Charbit, and Lopes.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClique Number of Tournaments II (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.07481)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Mon Sep  7 13:35:48 2026 ... We also study the tournament analogue of the Gyárfás--Sumner conjecture. We construct new $\\vecχ$-bounding tournaments and thereby prove a conjecture of Aboulker, Aubian, Charbit, and Lopes stating that every class of tournaments with bounded twin-width is $\\vecχ$-bounded.\nTitle: Clique Number of Tournaments II\nAuthors: Guillaume Aubian, Samuel Coulomb\nDate: Mon Sep  7 13:35:48 2026\n\nThe directed clique number $\\vecω(T)$ of a tournament $T$ is the minimum, over all orderings of the vertices of $T$, of the clique number of the graph whose edges are the arcs that point backward with respect to the ordering. In this paper, we prove that, for every integer $k \\geq 3$, deciding whether $\\vecω(T) \\leq k$ is NP-complete. This answers a question of Nguyen, Scott, and Seymour, and contrasts with the classical undirected setting, where deciding whether $ω(G) \\leq k$ is polynomial-time solvable for every fixed integer $k$. On the other hand, we give a polynomial-time algorithm distinguishing tournaments with $\\vecω(T) \\leq 2$ from those with $\\vecω(T) > 100$. We also study the tournament analogue of the Gyárfás--Sumner conjecture. We construct new $\\vecχ$-bounding tournaments and thereby prove a conjecture of Aboulker, Aubian, Charbit, and Lopes stating that every class of tournaments with bounded twin-width is $\\vecχ$-bounded. We then exhibit new tournaments that are not $\\vecχ$-bounding, disproving another conjecture of Aboulker, Aubian, Charbit, and Lopes, as well as two conjectures of Kim. Finally, we present infinite families of 3-$\\vecω$-critical and 4-$\\vecω$-critical tournaments.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIsomorphism for Tournaments of Small Twin Width (https://theoretics.episciences.org/17560/pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; tournament isomorphism, twin ... ments of bounded or moderately growing twin width is in polynomial time. ... TheoretiCS, Volume 5 (2026), Article 4, 1-37.\n1 / 37\n202 6 : 4\nReceived Aug 14, 2024\nRevised Nov 12, 2025\nAccepted Feb 8, 2026\nPublished Feb 21, 2026\nIsomorphism for\nTournaments of Small Twin\nWidth\nKey words and phrases\ntournament isomorphism, twin\nwidth, fixed-parameter tractability,\nWeisfeiler-Leman algorithm\nMartin Grohe a \u0000 \u001a\na RWTH Aachen University,\nAachen, Germany\nDaniel Neuen b \u0000 \u001a\nb TU Dresden, Dresden, Germany\nA B S T R AC T. We prove that isomorphism of tournaments of twin width at most 𝑘can be\ndecided in time 𝑘𝑂(log 𝑘)𝑛𝑂(1). This implies that the isomorphism problem for classes of tourna-\nments of bounded or moderately growing twin width is in polynomial time. By comparison,\nthere are classes of undirected graphs of bounded twin width that are isomorphism complete,\nthat is, the isomorphism problem for the classes is as hard as the general graph isomorphism\nproblem. Twin width is a graph parameter that has been introduced only recently (Bonnet et\nal., J. ACM 2022), but has received a lot of attention in structural graph theory since then. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFirst order logic and twin-width in tournaments and dense oriented graphs - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0195669825001362)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2025.104247 Get rights and content ... Twin-width is also characterised by three infinite families of obstructions: \\mathcal{T}$\\mathcal{T}$ has bounded twin-width if and only if it excludes at least one tournament from each family.\n\n## European Journal of Combinatorics\n\nVolume 132, Part B, February 2026, 104247\n\n# First order logic and twin-width in tournaments and dense oriented graphs☆\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.ejc.2025.104247Get rights and content\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe characterise the classes of tournaments with tractable first-order model checking. For every hereditary class of tournaments \\mathcal{T}$\\mathcal{T}$, first-order model checking is either fixed parameter tractable or \\text{AW}[\\ast ]$\\text{AW}[\\ast ]$-hard. This dichotomy coincides with the fact that \\mathcal{T}$\\mathcal{T}$ has either bounded or unbounded twin-width, and that the growth of \\mathcal{T}$\\mathcal{T}$ is either at most exponential or at least factorial. From the model-theoretic point of view, we show that NIP classes of tournaments coincide with bounded twin-width. Twin-width is also characterised by three infinite families of obstructions: \\mathcal{T}$\\mathcal{T}$ has bounded twin-width if and only if it excludes at least one tournament from each family. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClique Number of Tournaments II - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2609.07481v1)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; Published: 2026-09-07 ... We also study the tournament analogue of the Gyárfás--Sumner conjecture. We construct new $\\vecχ$-bounding tournaments and thereby prove a conjecture of Aboulker, Aubian, Charbit, and Lopes stating that every class of tournaments with bounded twin-width is $\\vecχ$-bounded.\n\n## Clique Number of Tournaments II\n\nGuillaume Aubian, Samuel Coulomb\n\nPublished: 2026-09-07\n\n\ncs.DM math.CO\n\nclique number graph np-complete tournament\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe directed clique number $\\vecω(T)$ of a tournament $T$ is the minimum, over all orderings of the vertices of $T$, of the clique number of the graph whose edges are the arcs that point backward with respect to the ordering. In this paper, we prove that, for every integer $k \\geq 3$, deciding whether $\\vecω(T) \\leq k$ is NP-complete. This answers a question of Nguyen, Scott, and Seymour, and contrasts with the classical undirected setting, where deciding whether $ω(G) \\leq k$ is polynomial-time solvable for every fixed integer $k$. On the other hand, we give a polynomial-time algorithm distinguishing tournaments with $\\vecω(T) \\leq 2$ from those with $\\vecω(T) > 100$. We also study the tournament analogue of the Gyárfás--Sumner conjecture. We construct new $\\vecχ$-bounding tournaments and thereby prove a conjecture of Aboulker, Aubian, Charbit, and Lopes stating that every class of tournaments with bounded twin-width is $\\vecχ$-bounded. We then exhibit new tournaments that are not $\\vecχ$-bounding, disproving another conjecture of Aboulker, Aubian, Charbit, and Lopes, as well as two conjectures of Kim. Finally, we present infinite families of 3-$\\vecω$-critical and 4-$\\vecω$-critical tournaments.\n\n### BibTeX\n\n[Button: arXiv BibTeX ] [Button: Published BibTeX ]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n\nLoading...\n\n[Button: Copy to Clipboard]\n\nMaintained by Per Alexandersson, per@symmetricfunctions.com--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFPT Isomorphism Test for $F$-Free Tournaments (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.24804)\nciteturn2academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Our result extends on a previous FPT isomorphism test for tournaments of bounded twin-width [Grohe, Neuen 2024], as well as XP isomorphism tests parameterized by the VC dimension or the chromatic number [Raßmann, Schweitzer 2026].\nTitle: FPT Isomorphism Test for $F$-Free Tournaments\nAuthors: Daniel Neuen\nDate: Mon Sep 21 16:01:44 2026\n\nWe show that isomorphism of $F$-free tournaments can be solved in FPT time $f(k) \\cdot n^{O(1)}$, where $k$ denotes the size of $F$, and $n$ denotes the size of the input tournaments. Our result extends on a previous FPT isomorphism test for tournaments of bounded twin-width [Grohe, Neuen 2024], as well as XP isomorphism tests parameterized by the VC dimension or the chromatic number [Raßmann, Schweitzer 2026]. It also implies that every non-trivial hereditary class of tournaments admits a polynomial-time isomorphism test. Our algorithm builds on a novel combination of spectral, geometric, combinatorial and group-theoretic tools.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHypercube geodesics with few colour changes (https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.20184)\nciteturn2academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Date: Tue May 19 17:59:50 2026 ... A conjecture of Norine, in a form due to Feder and Subi, states that this maximum should be 1. ... We improve this bound and answer a question of Leader and Long by finding a geodesic path with at most $(\\tfracπ{2} + o(1))\\sqrt{n}$ colour changes.\nTitle: Hypercube geodesics with few colour changes\nAuthors: Lawrence Hollom\nDate: Tue May 19 17:59:50 2026\n\nWhat is the maximum, over all 2-colourings of the edges of the $n$-dimensional hypercube $Q_n$, of the minimal number of times a path between a vertex $v$ and its antipode $\\bar{v}$ changes colour? A conjecture of Norine, in a form due to Feder and Subi, states that this maximum should be 1. The previous best-known upper bound on the number of colour changes was $(\\tfrac{3}{8} + o(1))n$ due to Dvořák. We improve this bound and answer a question of Leader and Long by finding a geodesic path with at most $(\\tfracπ{2} + o(1))\\sqrt{n}$ colour changes. In fact, we show that this is the expected number of colour changes for a uniformly random start vertex. This is optimal (up to the constant) when the start vertex is chosen uniformly at random.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFirst Order Logic and Twin-Width in Tournaments (https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol274-esa2023/LIPIcs.ESA.2023.53/LIPIcs.ESA.2023.53.pdf)\nciteturn2search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; model-theoretic point of view, we show that NIP classes of tournaments coincide with bounded ... Twin-width is also characterised by three infinite families of obstructions: T has bounded\ntwin-width if and only if it excludes at least one tournament from each family.\nFirst Order Logic and Twin-Width in Tournaments\nColin Geniet #\nUniv Lyon, CNRS, ENS de Lyon, Université Claude Bernard Lyon 1, LIP UMR5668, France\nStéphan Thomassé #\nUniv Lyon, CNRS, ENS de Lyon, Université Claude Bernard Lyon 1, LIP UMR5668, France\nAbstract\nWe characterise the classes of tournaments with tractable first-order model checking. For every\nhereditary class of tournaments T , first-order model checking either is fixed parameter tractable, or\nis AW[∗]-hard. This dichotomy coincides with the fact that T has either bounded or unbounded\ntwin-width, and that the growth of T is either at most exponential or at least factorial. From the\nmodel-theoretic point of view, we show that NIP classes of tournaments coincide with bounded\ntwin-width. Twin-width is also characterised by three infinite families of obstructions: T has bounded\ntwin-width if and only if it excludes at least one tournament from each family. This generalises\nresults of Bonnet et al. on ordered graphs.\nThe key for these results is a polynomial time algorithm which takes as input a tournament T\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBounded Twin-Width Tournaments are $\\dchi$-Bounded | Cool Papers - Immersive Paper Discovery (https://papers.cool/arxiv/2609.02763)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; For a tournament $T$ and a vertex ordering $\\prec$, let $T^{\\prec}$ be the graph of backward arcs in $\\prec$. ... Together with polynomial $χ$-boundedness of graphs of bounded twin-width, this implies that tournaments of bounded twin-width are $\\dchi$-bounded by $\\domega$, resolving a conjecture of Aboulker, Aubian, Charbit, and Lopes. ... Publish: 2026-09-02 16:04:27 UTC\n# 2609.02763\n\nTotal: 1\n\n## #1 Bounded Twin-Width Tournaments are $\\dchi$-Bounded [PDF^{}] [Copy] [Kimi^{}] [REL]\n\nAuthors: Chaoliang Tang, Junchi Zhang\n\nFor a tournament $T$ and a vertex ordering $\\prec$, let $T^{\\prec}$ be the graph of backward arcs in $\\prec$. The diclique number of a tournament is $\\domega(T)=\\min_{\\prec}ω(T^{\\prec})$, and the dichromatic number is