h-colouringshypercube\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRank-three case of the Kajitani–Ueno–Miyano cyclic orderability conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/405435/rank-three-case-of-the-kajitani-ueno-miyano-cyclic-orderabil)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; The Kajitani–Ueno–Miyano conjecture, posed in 1988, asks whether uniform density guarantees a cyclic ordering whose consecutive triples are bases. ... Current status (as of September 2026): The coprime, simple, and split subclasses are established; the remaining divisible nonsimple case is claimed solved by Fletcher but remains unverified. ... This proves that every uniformly dense rank-three matroid on $3k$3 k elements is cyclically orderable, and hence, together with the coprime theorem of van den Heuvel and Thomassé, establishes that every finite uniformly dense rank-three matroid is cyclically orderable.\n\nPosted by Austen FPosted 1 month ago · edited 2 weeks ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Refreshed 1 month ago\n\nOpen · solution reported\n\nA 2026 proof claims to settle the three-dimensional case, but no independent verification has been found.\n\nThe Kajitani–Ueno–Miyano conjecture, posed in 1988, asks whether uniform density guarantees a cyclic ordering whose consecutive triples are bases. Before the new claim, the coprime case, all simple rank-three cases, and a split-matroid subclass were known.\n\nSeptember 2026 claimed resolution\n\nA report and repository attribute to Austen Fletcher a theorem covering the remaining divisible case $|E|=3k$∣E∣=3 k, including nonsimple matroids.\n\nCommunity submission (unverified)\n\nA September 15, 2026 submission argues that the divisible theorem follows by separating proper tight flats from the strictly uniformly dense case, deleting a basis, and reinserting it via a two-gap lemma. This argument has not been checked here.\n\nCurrent status (as of September 2026): The coprime, simple, and split subclasses are established; the remaining divisible nonsimple case is claimed solved by Fletcher but remains unverified.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMonadic dependence from reducts, and applications to twin-width of oriented graphs - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2606.18934v1)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Published: 2026-06-17 ... First, generalizing the fact that twin-width boundedness is equivalent to being expandable by a linear order into a monadically dependent class, we prove that it is also equivalent to being expandable by an oriented graph with bounded independence number (for instance by a poset with bounded width or by a tournament), and that FO-model checking is fixed-parameter tractable on such an expansion.\n\n## Monadic dependence from reducts, and applications to twin-width of oriented graphs\n\nHector Buffière, Yuquan Lin, Patrice Ossona de Mendez\n\nPublished: 2026-06-17\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\ncs.DM cs.LO math.CO math.LO\n\ngraph independence number linear order poset tournament\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe study monadic dependence of binary relational structures including at least one antisymmetric relation. Our cornerstone result gives sufficient conditions for proving that a structure is monadically dependent by only considering some of its reducts, assuming they are structurally well-behaved and compatible enough. As an application, we consider some reorientation rules preserving monadic dependence of binary structures, as well as replacement of one antisymmetric relation with bounded independence number by another. Then, we apply our main (technical) result to the study of twin-width in two ways. First, generalizing the fact that twin-width boundedness is equivalent to being expandable by a linear order into a monadically dependent class, we prove that it is also equivalent to being expandable by an oriented graph with bounded independence number (for instance by a poset with bounded width or by a tournament), and that FO-model checking is fixed-parameter tractable on such an expansion. Second, we show delineation by twin-width for some new classes of oriented graphs, including oriented split graphs and local tournaments. In all these cases, we also obtain fixed-parameter tractability of FO-model checking.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPublikationen – Didaktik – TU Darmstadt (https://www.mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de/didaktik/ag_forschung/gg_forschung/publikationen/Publikationen.de.jsp)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; ### Isomorphism of tournaments with bounded VC dimension. ... ### Weisfeiler-Leman on graphs of small twin-width. ... WG 2026. ... Discrete Applied Mathematics 2025.\n## Publikationen der Arbeitsgruppe\n\n### Isomorphism of tournaments with bounded VC dimension.\n\nSimon Raßmann, and Pascal Schweitzer.\nPreprint. ArXiv\n\n### Randomization Helps in Online Graph Exploration: Breaking the Deterministic Lower Bound on Cycles.\n\nJúlia Baligács, Jan Hązła, and Lena Volk.\nPreprint. ArXiv\n\n### Weisfeiler-Leman on graphs of small twin-width.\n\nIrene Heinrich, Moritz Lichter, Klara Pakhomenko, and Simon Raßmann.\nWG 2026. DOI ArXiv\n\n### On Edge-Disjoint Maximal Outerplanar Graphs.\n\nYuto Okada, Yota Otachi, and Lena Volk.\nPreprint. ArXiv\n\n### \n\n### Finding a Maximum Common (Induced) Subgraph: Structural Parameters Revisited.\n\nTesshu Hanaka, Yuto Okada, Yota Otachi, and Lena Volk.\nWALCOM 2026. DOI ArXiv\n\n### \n\n### An Upper Bound on the Weisfeiler-Leman Dimens