strates the special\nrole of the tournament isomorphism problem, though here the reason is not group-theoretic,\nbut purely combinatorial.\nNote that the dependence on the twin width k of the algorithm in Theorem 1.1 is\nsubexponential, so our result implies that TI is in polynomial time even for tournaments\nof twin width 2O(√\nlog n). Since twin width is functionally smaller than clique width, our\nresult implies that TI is also fixed-parameter tractable when parameterized by clique width.\nAdditionally, we prove that the twin width of a tournament is functionally smaller than\nits directed tree width, a graph parameter originally introduced in [31]. Since the directed\ntree width of every directed graph is smaller than its cut width or directed path width,\nthe same also holds for these two parameters. Hence, TI is fixed-parameter tractable also\nwhen parameterized by directed tree width, directed path width or cut width. To the best\nof our knowledge, this was not known for any of these parameters. The fact that twin\nwidth is functionally smaller than directed tree width, directed path width and cut width on\ntournaments is interesting in its own right, because this result does not extend to general\ndirected graphs (for any of the three parameters).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Isomorphism for Tournaments of Small Twin Width (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/401102088_Isomorphism_for_Tournaments_of_Small_Twin_Width)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * February 2026 ... This implies that the isomorphism problem for classes of tournaments of bounded or moderately growing twin width is in polynomial time. ... tournament isomorphism, twin ... from Lemma 5.6.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Monochromatic Paths and a Topological Approach to Norine's Conjecture (https://www.researchgate.net/publ